  <article class="eu-prose">
    <p class="eu-kicker">Guide</p>
    <h1>How to write a national motto</h1>
    <p class="eu-lead">A motto is the shortest thing a country says about itself. The good ones are three or four words that have outlived the governments that chose them.</p>

    <h2>Keep it short</h2>
    <p>
      Most national mottos are under six words. France manages three: <i lang="fr">Liberté, égalité, fraternité</i>. A motto has to fit on a coin, a seal and a
      schoolroom wall; if yours needs a comma and a subordinate clause, it is a mission statement.
    </p>

    <h2>Say one thing</h2>
    <p>
      A motto names what holds the country together: unity, faith, freedom, endurance. Belgium's <i lang="fr">L'union fait la force</i> ("unity makes strength")
      is one idea, and a telling one for a country of several languages. Pick the tension your nation lives with, and take a side.
    </p>

    <h2>Choose the language on purpose</h2>
    <p>
      Latin lends age; a living language lends ownership; an old local language lends roots. Whichever you choose tells readers who the motto is for. The
      <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a> explains the terms.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>Open the <a href="/">maker</a>, or start from one of the <a href="/templates/">templates</a>. When the plate looks right, give it a country: <a href="https://eurth.org">Eurth</a> has a world for it to live in.</p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
